WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.560
How can we create internalized lasting
motivation for every day "should"

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:08.429
behaviors? These are the things people
know they should be doing but just

00:00:08.429 --> 00:00:13.950
struggle to muster the motivation to do
on a regular basis. These images give you

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:19.590
examples of the many such behaviors: save for the future, donate to

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:23.160
charity, read to your kids if you're a
parent, eat healthfully, take care of the

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:32.040
environment, vote, don't text while you're
driving, that sort of thing. And they also

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:36.660
represent, by the way, many of the pretty
serious and persistent social and policy

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:42.149
challenges that could be improved pretty
dramatically if we could find scalable

00:00:42.149 --> 00:00:46.890
ways, scalable and and low enough cost
that we could afford to scale them, to

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:53.190
get people to change their behavior. Now
I'm not obviously close to the first

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:56.370
person to realize that we need to get
people to change their behavior. These

00:00:56.370 --> 00:01:02.070
are just a couple of the
huge number of examples of appeals

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:06.689
people see every day to change their
behavior.

00:01:06.689 --> 00:01:13.590
You can exercise. There's medicine. You can
beat diabetes by eating a healthy diet.

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:18.180
we can save the environment by turning
off the lights. One thing I want you to

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:20.790
notice, the main thing I want you to
notice about these, is that these are all

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:24.090
pragmatic appeals, right? They focus on
the self-interested or otherwise

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:30.720
pragmatic reason why people should
change their behavior. And the problem

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:34.950
with that is that's telling the reader
why the person who made the appeal

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:38.670
thinks it's important. Or probably more
accurately, the person who commissioned

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:44.750
the appeal. But the problem
with that is that it it relies on

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:48.689
self-control or willpower to get people
to change their behavior, and

00:01:48.689 --> 00:01:53.189
psychologists have known for some time
this is not a domain you want to be

00:01:53.189 --> 00:01:59.430
relying on, right? Self-control and
willpower works sometimes in acute

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:07.350
situations. It does not work for
sustained behavior change. And so I've

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:11.190
been thinking a little bit about how to
sort of focus this question even

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:14.470
more and the sort of focused version of the

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:18.250
question that I've been focusing on is:
how can we get people to care about the

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:23.350
things they don't already care about but
should? A couple of things I want you to

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:28.450
sort of keep in mind about "should"
behaviors. One is this doesn't represent

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:31.460
the situation. People know what the
responsible choice is. They're not

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:35.980
in doubt about that, right?
And so telling them or reminding them of

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:39.760
the pragmatic reasons why they should
change their behavior is unlikely to be

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:43.870
giving them new information, so it's not
really clear even what the theory of

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:47.730
change is behind a pragmatic appeal.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:55.540
Other things that are a little
less directly obvious are our

00:02:55.540 --> 00:03:00.460
problems like myopia, or intertemporal
discounting. It's referred to in varying

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:04.930
ways depending on whether you're talking
to a psychologist or an economist. But

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:10.450
the central idea there is that we over
value the immediate present and

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:17.020
undervalue the distant future. And so as
with many "should" behaviors, when a

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:21.550
behavior requires a sacrifice in the
immediate term in service of some

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:26.680
distant pragmatic goal, the distant
pragmatic goal is unlikely to win that

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:31.720
fight. Relatedly but distinct is the
notion of procrastination, which is

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:36.880
simply: even if I do care about that
distant pragmatic goal, there's rarely a

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:39.850
compelling reason for me to start
changing my behavior today rather than

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:46.020
tomorrow, right? And both of these pose
very serious challenges to

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:52.330
getting people to engage in "should"
behaviors. And then the third important

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:57.250
thing to remember, I guess it's the
fourth important thing I want to make

00:03:57.250 --> 00:04:01.420
top of mind for you is that positive
impact for many of these behaviors

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:07.060
depends on sustained repeated change in
behavior. And that's really important

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:11.860
because there's been a major and
important movement in behavior change

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:16.810
recently called called "nudge." And most
people refer to it as nudge, and it's the

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:22.600
idea that you can set up choice
environments so that the easiest thing

00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:26.380
to do is the thing that's in most
people's best interest.

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:30.580
And it's a huge innovation. I think
it's probably the single most important

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:34.840
innovation in the science of
behavior change, I don't know, maybe ever.

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:40.630
It's a hugely important innovation. But
the problem, the weakness of it is that

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:45.789
it's not very well suited to the sorts
of "should" behaviors that require

00:04:45.789 --> 00:04:49.389
sustained change across a wide range of
contexts, for the simple reason that we

00:04:49.389 --> 00:04:52.120
don't have control over enough of the
context in which people are making

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:55.120
decisions, right? If you're trying to get
people to adopt healthy diets, you might

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:59.020
be able to change the order of the menu
items at a restaurant, but that's not

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:01.810
going to transform very many people's
lives, because most people don't eat at

00:05:01.810 --> 00:05:09.789
one restaurant all the time. And so the
sort of complementary idea to nudge that

00:05:09.789 --> 00:05:14.020
I've been working on with my
collaborators is something I'm calling

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:20.139
'values alignment." And the idea is to
identify existing strongly-held and

00:05:20.139 --> 00:05:25.840
widely-shared values that the desired
behavior might serve. And then frame

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:30.370
appeals for that behavior in terms of
how they might serve this alternative

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:34.419
value. And so the idea is you're changing
how people think about the reason for

00:05:34.419 --> 00:05:38.860
engaging in "should" behaviors. It
becomes less about the distant pragmatic

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:43.570
outcome you're pursuing and more
about living in accordance with some

00:05:43.570 --> 00:05:51.159
value that is important to you in the
immediate term. And so the idea here,

00:05:51.159 --> 00:05:54.370
sort of from a theoretical perspective, I
talked about intertemporal discounting,

00:05:54.370 --> 00:05:57.880
this problem that the distant pragmatic
outcome just isn't a strong enough

00:05:57.880 --> 00:06:01.930
motivator for people. the idea of values
alignment is that you replace that

00:06:01.930 --> 00:06:05.830
distant pragmatic outcome with an
immediate symbolic outcome: the good

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:10.150
feeling of knowing you're being the kind
of person you feel you should

00:06:10.150 --> 00:06:15.759
be being. And when I say "values" I really
do mean values broadly construed, right?

00:06:15.759 --> 00:06:20.840
what a person thinks is important in
life. What has immediate motivational force.

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:30.269
 

